@echo-125/deh-remote README · Origin and scope：This plugin began as one package inside the `dsh-web` monorepo and was split into a
**standalone repository** in October 2026 (the shared build presets were inlined into
`build/`), then taken from "the phone can open it" to "it works on a real iPhone".

@echo-125/deh-remote README · Origin and scope：> Known open issue: after "Add workspace" on the phone, **the folder must be chosen on the
> computer**; the phone shows no feedback until then (the channel and the reply are
> both fine — see PROJECT_HISTORY.md §4.1).

@echo-125/deh-remote README · What it does：- **Entry**: a phone icon beside the settings button in both the expanded sidebar and the narrow rail; its tooltip and accessible label say "Remote access".
- **Panel**: "Remote access" title, a "Pair a device" card with the status area ("Waiting for a device" + status badge), a large QR code, the pairing link and token each with a copy button, Stop / Refresh QR actions, and the authorized-device roster (a device name inferred from User-Agent, online/offline, last active time, per-device unpair). Credential-bearing device ids and raw User-Agent values are never rendered. Both the phone and the PC use the same time-limited link: until it expires or the QR is refreshed, that link can pair several devices, each with its own device session.
- **Phone side**: scanning the QR binds the phone with a time-limited token and reloads into the **official Web GUI** — there is no second interface to drift from. While the phone is in portrait, the plugin injects a touch-adaptation layer over the running UI (see "The mobile adaptation layer" below). The accept chain is cookieless (`/pair-accept` → `/pair-app`): the plugin itself serves the official shell,

@echo-125/deh-remote README · Workspaces on a phone：Two consequences worth knowing: a conversation started from the phone has a folder
of its own (it does not land in "Ungrouped"), and picking a different folder is a
desktop action - the host owns that choice, not the phone.

@echo-125/deh-remote README · Diagnosing a connection：When a paired phone will not settle, the cause is usually on the wire rather than
in the UI, and a phone has no DevTools to look at. This plugin therefore carries
its own diagnosis, off by default:

@echo-125/deh-remote README · Diagnosing a connection：Turn it on with the **通道诊断 / Channel diagnostics** switch in the settings card.
The `?remoteDiag=1` query on the landing URL stays as the emergency entry that
needs no settings round trip. With the diagnostics off, nothing is written at all.

@echo-125/deh-remote README · The mobile adaptation layer：- a CSS sheet keyed on CSS-Modules **semantic suffixes** (`[class$="_composerSeat"]`) so the selectors survive official rebuilds that only change hashes: 44px touch targets on the collapsed rail, 16px inputs (prevents iOS focus zoom), safe-area padding for the composer, compact type for the message list and sidebar, a column layout for the settings modal, and a full-width PlanReview card;
- a **draggable whale button** as the entry to the collapsed sidebar (position persisted; wired to the official `ctx.layout.toggleSidebar()`), hidden while the sidebar is expanded;
- **gestures**: left-swipe collapses the sidebar, right-swipe in the conversation opens it; long-pressing a session row opens the same action menu as the desktop ellipsis; tapping a session row or anywhere outside the sidebar collapses it again;
- **input behavior for touch**: Enter only inserts a newline (send goes through the send button), programmatic composer focus is suppressed (no spurious keyboards), and official tooltip bubbles are hidden because taps leave them stuck;
